<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Culture and the arts in france and all around the world - RFI</title><description>All the cultural news on RFI. The latest information, news and artistic events all around the world.</description><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/</link><lastBuildDate>Wed, 19 Aug 2026 09:53:36 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Culture</category><title>Angélique Kidjo says Hollywood star is 'for Africa, but also for France'</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260819-ang%C3%A9lique-kidjo-says-hollywood-star-is-for-africa-but-also-for-france</link><description>
Singer Angélique Kidjo has become the first black African performer to receive a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame – telling RFI the honour represented “a door opening” at a time of global turmoil.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0a95e70a-9ba3-11f1-88f4-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26230754675930.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0a95e70a-9ba3-11f1-88f4-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26230754675930.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">be02e342-9ba8-11f1-8c16-c92f114b9305</guid><pubDate>Wed, 19 Aug 2026 09:27:19 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0a95e70a-9ba3-11f1-88f4-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26230754675930.jpg">AP Photo/Chris Pizzello - Chris Pizzello</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Uganda's Ghetto Kids to shake it with Shakira at World Cup final</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260614-uganda-s-ghetto-kids-to-shake-it-with-shakira-at-world-cup-final</link><description>
A group of young Ugandan dancers from the slums of Kampala have been invited to perform alongside pop star Shakira at the 2026 World Cup final, an opportunity they hope will bring them new visibility and help them transform their future.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ab0b54cc-642b-11f1-bafc-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/OUGANDA-DANSE-GHETTO-KIDS-COUPE-MONDE-FOOTBALL.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ab0b54cc-642b-11f1-bafc-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/OUGANDA-DANSE-GHETTO-KIDS-COUPE-MONDE-FOOTBALL.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c12bae4e-657c-11f1-9062-55a4c1261eb8</guid><pubDate>Sun, 14 Jun 2026 10:11:13 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ab0b54cc-642b-11f1-bafc-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/OUGANDA-DANSE-GHETTO-KIDS-COUPE-MONDE-FOOTBALL.jpg">AFP - BADRU KATUMBA</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>The project nurturing West Africa's new generation of women filmmakers</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260614-the-project-nurturing-west-africa-s-new-generation-of-women-filmmakers</link><description>
Some of the West African film industry's biggest names are backing a new mentorship programme to support emerging women filmmakers. Showcased at this year's Cannes Film Festival, the Mariama Lab project aims to help women bring their stories to a wider audience in the region and beyond.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/04a7a446-525d-11ea-ba40-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/Ang%C3%A8le%20Diabang_0.JPG"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/04a7a446-525d-11ea-ba40-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/Ang%C3%A8le%20Diabang_0.JPG" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a605529e-64cf-11f1-8913-879b1baa666e</guid><pubDate>Sun, 14 Jun 2026 05:28:21 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/04a7a446-525d-11ea-ba40-005056a964fe/w:1024/p:16x9/Ang%C3%A8le%20Diabang_0.JPG">© Karoninka</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>British art 'giant' David Hockney dies aged 88</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260612-british-art-giant-david-hockney-dies-aged-88</link><description>
British artist David Hockney, one of the most influential and defining figures in contemporary art, whose paintings captured the world in brilliant colour, has died aged 88, his publicist announced Friday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/81eaf58e-664c-11f1-96eb-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/af61319312ce5c2a8552f9ea4ab2f071dfca037d.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/81eaf58e-664c-11f1-96eb-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/af61319312ce5c2a8552f9ea4ab2f071dfca037d.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">82bfa7e8-664c-11f1-adf2-670ac5853964</guid><pubDate>Fri, 12 Jun 2026 10:50:21 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/81eaf58e-664c-11f1-96eb-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/af61319312ce5c2a8552f9ea4ab2f071dfca037d.jpg">© Martin BUREAU / AFP/File</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Sound of the silver screen: meet the man getting film music made</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260607-cannes-pierre-marie-dru-meet-the-man-getting-film-music-made</link><description>
Film music helps create the magic of cinema. But who works on this key creative process behind the scenes? French film music supervisor Pierre-Marie Dru tells RFI what it takes to match movies with the perfect soundtrack.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a0dc4a08-16b5-11ef-8fcf-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Zoe%20Saldana%20plays%20a%20lawyer%20in%20Emilia%20Perez%20by%20Jacques%20Audiard_Cannes%202024_Credit%20Shannon%20Besson.JPG"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a0dc4a08-16b5-11ef-8fcf-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Zoe%20Saldana%20plays%20a%20lawyer%20in%20Emilia%20Perez%20by%20Jacques%20Audiard_Cannes%202024_Credit%20Shannon%20Besson.JPG" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">13a1d6b4-5f36-11f1-80a1-7932bbcece50</guid><pubDate>Sun, 07 Jun 2026 12:02:39 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a0dc4a08-16b5-11ef-8fcf-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Zoe%20Saldana%20plays%20a%20lawyer%20in%20Emilia%20Perez%20by%20Jacques%20Audiard_Cannes%202024_Credit%20Shannon%20Besson.JPG">© Shanna Besson</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Cinema should be 'a place for everyone': Cannes rising star Anna Cazenave Cambet</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260531-cinema-should-be-a-place-for-everyone-cannes-rising-star-anna-cazenave-cambet</link><description>
French director Anna Cazenave Cambet first came to Cannes in 2016 as a student with a short film called Gabber Lover, which won the Queer Palm award. With two feature films under her belt, she returned to the Croisette in 2026 as one of the "10 to Watch" rising French stars. RFI spoke to her about finding her place as a young woman in the industry, and her hopes for the future of cinema.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/12303926-590d-11f1-ba4e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Mont%C3%A9e-des-marches-10-to-watch-group-20-mai-2026-Credit-Loic-Th%C3%A9baud-Unifrance.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/12303926-590d-11f1-ba4e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Mont%C3%A9e-des-marches-10-to-watch-group-20-mai-2026-Credit-Loic-Th%C3%A9baud-Unifrance.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ea5f7fb0-5907-11f1-a0c7-8ff925d54c85</guid><pubDate>Sun, 31 May 2026 10:00:55 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/12303926-590d-11f1-ba4e-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Mont%C3%A9e-des-marches-10-to-watch-group-20-mai-2026-Credit-Loic-Th%C3%A9baud-Unifrance.jpg">© Loïc Thébaud / Unifrance</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>‘Forgiveness has to be lived out’: Rwanda's Cannes winner Dusabejambo</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260525-forgiveness-has-to-be-lived-out-says-rwanda-s-cannes-laureat-dusabejambot</link><description>
Rwandan director Marie-Clémentine Dusabejambo has become the country's first filmmaker to win the Camera d’Or for best first feature at the Cannes film festival, with Ben’Imana. The Kigali-based director talks to RFI about exploring how survivors and perpetrators continued living side by side in the year’s following the 1994 genocide and the difficult but necessary path towards reconciliation and healing.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/91d52c96-575e-11f1-9b9e-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Marie-Clementine-IP-DSCF4317.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/91d52c96-575e-11f1-9b9e-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Marie-Clementine-IP-DSCF4317.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ddc0ac42-581d-11f1-ad1b-05bf8ed4411d</guid><pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 14:32:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/91d52c96-575e-11f1-9b9e-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Marie-Clementine-IP-DSCF4317.jpg">© Siegfried Forster / RFI</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Romanian director wins Palme d'Or at emotional Cannes ceremony</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260524-romanian-director-wins-palme-d-or-at-emotional-cannes-ceremony</link><description>
The 79th edition of the Cannes Film Festival came to a close on Saturday evening, with the coveted Palme d’Or for best film going to family drama Fjord by Romanian director Cristian Mungiu. He called on filmmakers to set the example by promoting tolerance.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e6a07fb8-56ec-11f1-9581-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26143741587753.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e6a07fb8-56ec-11f1-9581-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26143741587753.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">427dbf22-56fb-11f1-b59e-a5309fde48e7</guid><pubDate>Sun, 24 May 2026 00:09:56 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e6a07fb8-56ec-11f1-9581-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26143741587753.jpg">© AP Photo/Scott A Garfitt </source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>From critic favourites to commercial hits, Japanese cinema wins fans at Cannes</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260523-from-critic-favourites-to-commercial-hits-japanese-cinema-wins-fans-at-cannes</link><description>
With no fewer than 12 films in the official selection, Japan is at the heart of this year's Cannes Festival. Its cinema industry continues to do well with moviegoers as well as critics, making it a notable success story amid the rise of streaming.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/965dd00c-560e-11f1-91cd-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Sur-le-tournage-de-Au-bout-du-monde-de-Kiyoshi-Kurosawa-UZBEKKINO.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/965dd00c-560e-11f1-91cd-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Sur-le-tournage-de-Au-bout-du-monde-de-Kiyoshi-Kurosawa-UZBEKKINO.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a79824e0-560d-11f1-a221-8de3818ef238</guid><pubDate>Sat, 23 May 2026 17:05:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/965dd00c-560e-11f1-91cd-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Sur-le-tournage-de-Au-bout-du-monde-de-Kiyoshi-Kurosawa-UZBEKKINO.jpg">© Uzbekkino</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Cannes Film Festival 2026: ‘It's on the US independent side that I see hope'</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260520-cannes-film-festival-2026-it-s-on-the-us-independent-side-that-i-see-hope</link><description>
While the absence of major Hollywood studios has caused a stir in Cannes this year, Eugene Hernandez, director of the Sundance Film Festival, founded by the late Robert Redford, told RFI why this needn't be cause for concern.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/32b94fde-52a0-11f1-a03c-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Pavillon-americain-IMG-0605.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/32b94fde-52a0-11f1-a03c-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Pavillon-americain-IMG-0605.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">baeaeba4-5455-11f1-8f24-ef8882901dbc</guid><pubDate>Wed, 20 May 2026 15:51:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/32b94fde-52a0-11f1-a03c-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Pavillon-americain-IMG-0605.jpg">© Siegfried Forster / RFI</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>From refugee to red carpet, the remarkable journey of Amine Benrachid</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260519-from-refugee-to-red-carpet-the-remarkable-journey-of-amine-benrachid</link><description>
Amine Benrachid arrived in France with nothing but the clothes on his back, having crossed the Mediterranean on a rubber dinghy and walked across the border on foot. Today, the Chadian-South Sudanese actor is sitting on the jury of the Citizenship Prize at Cannes, and is preparing to tell his own story on screen.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/684bea6e-5046-11f1-baa4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/WhatsApp-Image-2026-05-14-at-16-42-47.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/684bea6e-5046-11f1-baa4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/WhatsApp-Image-2026-05-14-at-16-42-47.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1a426a64-5370-11f1-b7ab-e761326535ac</guid><pubDate>Tue, 19 May 2026 14:44:55 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/684bea6e-5046-11f1-baa4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/WhatsApp-Image-2026-05-14-at-16-42-47.jpeg">© Houda Ibrahim/RFI</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Bulgaria wins Eurovision Song Contest, Israel comes second again</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260517-bulgaria-wins-eurovision-song-contest-israel-comes-second-again</link><description>
Bulgaria won the Eurovision Song Contest for the first time on Saturday in a final overshadowed by five countries' boycott over Gaza, claiming a dramatic victory despite another big public vote for Israel that again secured it second place. France finished in 11th place.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3553125c-517e-11f1-a4ed-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-16T232010Z-1308790745-RC2NALAMNUVL-RTRMADP-3-MUSIC-EUROVISION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3553125c-517e-11f1-a4ed-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-16T232010Z-1308790745-RC2NALAMNUVL-RTRMADP-3-MUSIC-EUROVISION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d72c3a10-51bd-11f1-b3e0-256157db24bc</guid><pubDate>Sun, 17 May 2026 07:33:36 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3553125c-517e-11f1-a4ed-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-16T232010Z-1308790745-RC2NALAMNUVL-RTRMADP-3-MUSIC-EUROVISION.jpg">© Lisa Leutner / Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Why is African cinema out of the picture at the Cannes Film Festival?</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260516-why-is-african-cinema-out-of-the-picture-at-the-cannes-film-festival</link><description>
Does the absence of African films in the main competition at this year’s Cannes Film Festival point to a glass ceiling? Or is African cinema still slowly but surely finding its way to the top table?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e3e4fe68-4d58-11f1-ac24-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Ben-Imana1-photo.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e3e4fe68-4d58-11f1-ac24-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Ben-Imana1-photo.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1eec8e60-4ed8-11f1-a9ed-15a474dc4e28</guid><pubDate>Sat, 16 May 2026 16:05:43 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/e3e4fe68-4d58-11f1-ac24-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Ben-Imana1-photo.jpg">© Festival de Cannes</source><dc:creator>Alison Hird with RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>France has 'responsibility' to break glass ceiling for women in film</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260515-france-has-responsibility-to-break-glass-ceiling-for-women-in-film</link><description>
Only five directors out of 22 at this year's Cannes Film Festival are women – two fewer than in 2025. Campaigners who want to see the film industry become more inclusive say the numbers reflect a broader gender gap in French cinema.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0c953544-4e94-11f1-8b52-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26132624063724-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0c953544-4e94-11f1-8b52-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26132624063724-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5342cb8c-4edf-11f1-a4aa-b9216e3017ef</guid><pubDate>Fri, 15 May 2026 09:41:01 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0c953544-4e94-11f1-8b52-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26132624063724-1.jpg">© Scott A Garfitt/Invision/AP</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Cannes Film Festival off to 'fast and furious' start despite Hollywood's absence</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260514-cannes-film-festival-off-to-fast-and-furious-start-despite-hollywood-s-absence</link><description>
The Cannes Film Festival revved up its engines Wednesday evening with a screening of the 2001 blockbuster that launched the Fast and Furious franchise – a tribute to Hollywood despite a notable absence of large studios this year. Meanwhile, the main competition got started with films from Japan and France.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90e38d6e-4f75-11f1-8b78-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Vin-Diesel-and-cast-of-Fast-and-Furious-at-the-Cannes-Film-Festival-13-May-2026-AFP-Thibaud-Moritz.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90e38d6e-4f75-11f1-8b78-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Vin-Diesel-and-cast-of-Fast-and-Furious-at-the-Cannes-Film-Festival-13-May-2026-AFP-Thibaud-Moritz.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">22db5cbc-4f74-11f1-92bf-d33775016048</guid><pubDate>Thu, 14 May 2026 14:12:33 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90e38d6e-4f75-11f1-8b78-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Vin-Diesel-and-cast-of-Fast-and-Furious-at-the-Cannes-Film-Festival-13-May-2026-AFP-Thibaud-Moritz.jpg">© AFP - THIBAUD MORITZ </source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Cannes promises ‘electric’ opener, as AI fears feature behind the scenes</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260512-cannes-promises-electric-opening-night-as-ai-fears-feature-behind-the-scenes</link><description>
The Cannes Film Festival on Tuesday unrolled its red carpet, with 22 films vying for the big prize – the Palme d'Or. Meanwhile behind the scenes, artificial intelligence and the absence of major American studios are what's got everyone talking.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bfe3c12-4de4-11f1-b658-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2KN4RU.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bfe3c12-4de4-11f1-b658-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2KN4RU.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1b6ff51a-4e01-11f1-94fb-8b48a83daee4</guid><pubDate>Tue, 12 May 2026 16:06:28 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5bfe3c12-4de4-11f1-b658-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2KN4RU.jpg">AFP - THIBAUD MORITZ</source><dc:creator>Ollia Horton with RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Culture</category><title>Malian filmmaker captures disappearing Tuareg culture and traditions</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/culture/20260510-tuareg-history-documentary-highlights-a-vanishing-culture-malian-el-ansari</link><description>
In Undertows: A Tuareg Tale, Malian film director Intagrist el Ansari captures the way of life of his people – the Tuaregs. Filmed over 10 years in nomadic settlements and refugee camps, the documentary bears witness to a world "that no longer quite exists".
